{"chunk_id": "66c45aa8b07f", "text": "This section of the Mahabharata continues the dialogue between Pandu and Kunti about how to obtain children despite Pandu\u2019s inability to beget them. Pandu recounts older social customs, claiming that in ancient times women moved freely and were not bound exclusively to one husband. He explains that strict marital fidelity became law only after the sage Swetaketu, angered by seeing his mother taken by another man according to earlier custom, imposed a new rule. Pandu then cites precedents where wives conceived through other men for the sake of lineage, including Madayanti and even the Kuru line itself. Appealing humbly to Kunti, he asks her to bear children through a worthy Brahmana. Kunti responds by revealing a secret boon: a mantra given by the sage Durvasa that can summon any god to grant her offspring, setting the stage for the divine births of the Pandavas.\nPandu explains ancient marital customs and asks Kunti to obtain offspring through another man; Kunti reveals a mantra to summon gods for conception.\nThemes: marriage customs, niyoga and lineage, moral law vs tradition, ascetic power, divine conception", "vector": [0.014383405447006226, -0.0226335097104311, 0.0004893410950899124, -0.0009218609193339944, 0.0017037889920175076, 0.020855937153100967, -0.006421647500246763, -0.00037591063301078975, -0.03435734286904335, -0.027342040091753006, 0.011255692690610886, 0.01413915865123272, 0.003663697512820363, 0.019404027611017227, -0.009559537284076214, 0.022036463022232056, 0.04429003223776817, -0.019186919555068016, 0.026853546500205994, -0.02446535788476467, -0.013040049932897091, -0.020760953426361084, 0.0107536306604743, -0.00896927434951067, -0.023814033716917038, 0.022714925929903984, 0.029960904270410538, -0.022606370970606804, 0.005916193127632141, -0.013582819141447544, -0.011079292744398117, 0.014994021505117416, -0.003461854998022318, -0.017517901957035065, -0.03503580391407013, -0.014763344079256058, -0.00209